course: dictionary
lesson: 2557
title: Delete in context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To delete means to take away or wipe out something, like a photo, a file or a word. Context helps you check the meaning. «Theo typed a long nasty message, read it back, deleted every word and sent "gg" instead.» Theo took the words away and sent something short, so deleted must mean removed.
explain: In writing, to delete is to cross out or remove words. «Deleting a whole project throws away the good parts too.» Here delete means wiping out the whole thing, so the good parts go too. Swap in "remove" and the sentence still makes sense, so you know you have the right meaning.
explain: Delete is also a maths word. Taking away a file, a photo or a song makes the number go down, which is subtraction. Online, deleting something does not always mean it is gone everywhere. «Things posted online can be copied, shared and kept for years, even after you delete them.»
fact: To delete means to take away or wipe out something, like a photo, a file or a word.
fact: In writing, to delete means to cross out or remove words.
fact: «Deleted» is the past form: «Theo deleted every word.»
fact: Deleting a photo or a file takes it away, so the number of files goes down, like subtraction.
fact: Things posted online can be copied and kept for years, even after you delete them.
exercise: «Theo typed a long nasty message, then deleted every word.» What does deleted mean here? | Read it back out loud | Made it longer | *Took away every word | Sent it to a friend
exercise: «Deleting a whole project throws away the good parts too.» What does deleting mean here? | Adding more parts | Printing it | *Wiping out the whole project | Copying it onto a new page
exercise: In writing, what does it mean to delete a word? | *Cross it out or remove it | Read it twice | Write it in capitals | Save it in a new file
test: Which action would make the number of photos on your tablet go down? | Adding a new photo | Sharing a photo | *Deleting a photo | Printing a photo
test: Why might a deleted post still be found online? | Photos are never shared | Deleting always destroys every copy for ever | Only teachers can see posts | *Things posted online can be copied and kept for years
exam: Priya asks, «What does delete mean when I cross out a word?» | *To cross out or remove words | To copy the words | To add words at the end | To read the words aloud
